{#
    One page in the rail, and its children if it has any.

    The current page is the one whose link the renderer resolves to `#`: it
    generates a URL relative to the page being rendered, and the page's own
    URL is nothing. `isCurrent` is not set on these nodes, so this is the
    signal there is — and it is the same one the top navigation reads.

    A page with children is a `<details>`, open when the reader is inside it,
    which is a question the summary cannot ask and the children can: if any of
    them is the current page, the fold is open.

    The chevron is the fold's only affordance — the browser's own triangle is
    taken away by the stylesheet — so a summary written without one is a
    heading nobody can tell is a control. It is the same markup `sds-rail`
    renders, and it has to be, or the two drift.
#}
{%- if node.children|length -%}
    {%- set open = node.children|filter(child => renderLink(child.url) == '#')|length > 0 -%}
    <details class="sds-rail__group"{% if open or node.isInRootline %} open{% endif %}>
        <summary><sds-icon name="actions-chevron-down"></sds-icon>{{ node.value.toString }}</summary>
        {%- set url = renderLink(node.url) -%}
        <a class="sds-rail__item{% if url == '#' %} is-active{% endif %}"
           href="{{ url }}"{% if url == '#' %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ node.value.toString }}</a>
        {%- for child in node.children -%}
            {{ renderNode(child) }}
        {%- endfor -%}
    </details>
{%- else -%}
    {%- set url = renderLink(node.url) -%}
    <a class="sds-rail__item{% if url == '#' %} is-active{% endif %}"
       href="{{ url }}"{% if url == '#' %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ node.value.toString }}</a>
{%- endif -%}
